L(1, '1. Separate chosen settings from learned state',
  'Inspect nested parameters and clone an unfitted copy without carrying fitted coefficients.',
  [('Hyperparameter; C', 'A setting supplied before fitting. LogisticRegression C controls inverse regularization strength: smaller positive C usually means stronger regularization.'),
   ('get_params; set_params; clone', 'Inspect or set estimator configuration; clone copies that configuration into an unfitted estimator. A double underscore addresses a named nested step.')],
  'Parameters such as C are chosen before a particular fit. Coefficients, imputation medians and scaling means are learned during that fit. In scikit-learn, fitted attributes conventionally end in an underscore. Copying configuration is different from copying learned state.\n\nA pipeline exposes the model step through model__C. You can therefore search settings while each candidate receives its own freshly fitted preparation. Do not change a fitted object and assume its old coefficients now correspond to the new configuration: fit the new candidate.\n\nThe example clones a fitted training pipeline. The clone keeps C but has no learned classes. It has not earned a score yet. The final holdout remains outside all examples on this day.',
  FIXED47 + 'from sklearn.base import clone\nm.set_params(model__C=0.1)\nm.fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\ncopy = clone(m)\nprint("Original learned:", hasattr(m.named_steps["model"], "classes_"))\nprint("Clone C:", copy.get_params()["model__C"])\nprint("Clone learned:", hasattr(copy.named_steps["model"], "classes_"))',
  'Inspect three configuration values without fitting.',
  'from lab import make_pipeline\np = make_pipeline(C=0.1)\nfor key in ["model__C", "model__class_weight", "fill__strategy"]:\n    print(key, p.get_params()[key])',
  'Fit one of two clones and verify that the other stays unfitted.',
  'from sklearn.base import clone\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_pipeline\nd = make_splits()\na, b = clone(make_pipeline()), clone(make_pipeline())\na.fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Separate learned state:", hasattr(a.named_steps["model"], "classes_") and not hasattr(b.named_steps["model"], "classes_"))',
  [('Does clone retain fitted coefficients?', 'No; it copies configuration', 'Always', 'Only with a fixed seed', 'Each fold must learn its own state.'),
   ('What does model__C address?', 'C on the named model step', 'A column named C', 'A previously learned coefficient', 'Double underscores traverse estimator configuration.')],
  'Treat a cloned or reconfigured estimator as already fitted.', 'Fit every configured candidate on its permitted fitting rows.',
  'The original has learned classes, while the cloned configuration has not. The final holdout was not used.')

L(1, '2. Give grid search a budget and a selection score',
  'Count candidate fits and distinguish best inner feedback from independent evaluation.',
  [('GridSearchCV; ParameterGrid', 'GridSearchCV evaluates every declared parameter combination across its inner folds. ParameterGrid enumerates those combinations.'),
   ('best_params_; best_score_; refit', 'The winning settings and their mean inner score. With refit=True, the winner is fitted again on all rows supplied to the search, not on the reserved holdout.')],
  'A grid is a finite list of choices, not a guarantee that the best possible model lies inside it. Three C values across three folds require nine candidate fits, followed by a full-input refit when enabled. Adding two class-weight settings doubles the candidate count.\n\nThis standalone interface exercise selects average precision. It is separate from the milestone, which declares false-positive and false-negative costs and tunes a decision threshold. Do not silently switch the release objective according to whichever score looks better.\n\nThe winning inner score helped select the winner. Use outer evaluation to assess that selection procedure. Keep the grid, score, random seeds and budget in the experiment record; otherwise a search result is difficult to reproduce or compare fairly.',
  'from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV, ParameterGrid, StratifiedKFold\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_pipeline\nd = make_splits()\ngrid = {"model__C": [0.1, 1, 10]}\ns = GridSearchCV(make_pipeline(), grid, cv=StratifiedKFold(3, shuffle=True, random_state=47), scoring="average_precision", n_jobs=1, error_score="raise")\ns.fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Candidates:", len(ParameterGrid(grid)))\nprint("Best settings:", s.best_params_)\nprint("Inner AP:", round(s.best_score_, 3))\nprint("Refit rows:", int(s.best_estimator_.named_steps["scale"].n_samples_seen_))',
  'Count a three-C, two-weight grid under three inner folds, excluding the final refit.',
  'from sklearn.model_selection import ParameterGrid\ngrid = {"model__C": [0.1, 1, 10], "model__class_weight": [None, "balanced"]}\nn = len(ParameterGrid(grid))\nprint("Candidates:", n, "candidate-fold fits:", n * 3)',
  'Label the role of a winning inner score.',
  'print("Best inner AP: selection feedback")\nprint("Outer validation: evaluation of the declared selection procedure")\nprint("Final holdout: frozen full-training report")',
  [('Does best_score_ automatically provide untouched evaluation?', 'No; it helped choose the winner', 'Always', 'Only for three folds', 'Search feedback is part of model development.'),
   ('Where does refit=True fit the winner?', 'On all rows supplied to search.fit', 'On the reserved final holdout', 'Only on the highest-scoring validation fold', 'The search input defines its permitted training scope.')],
  'Publish the best grid score as an independent test result.', 'Evaluate the entire search on held-out outer cases.',
  'The scaler row count verifies the final refit uses the 960 training rows. It does not demonstrate independent evaluation.')

L(1, '3. Sample a declared random-search space',
  'Use a fixed sampling budget and distinguish log-uniform choices from a linear grid.',
  [('RandomizedSearchCV; n_iter', 'Samples a specified number of candidate settings from declared distributions. A seed makes this sampling reproducible in the pinned environment.'),
   ('scipy.stats.loguniform', 'A positive distribution uniform on a logarithmic scale. Equal multiplicative ranges receive equal mass; useful when plausible regularization values span orders of magnitude.')],
  'A large Cartesian grid spends budget on every combination. Random search instead samples a limited number of combinations. It can explore continuous ranges without assuming that four particular grid points are sufficient. The chosen sampling range still encodes your assumptions.\n\nSampling C from 0.01 to 100 on a log scale treats 0.01–0.1 and 1–10 as equally wide ranges. This is different from uniform sampling of the raw number. Declare the distribution, number of trials and scoring rule before interpreting results.\n\nThe example again uses AP as an isolated search-interface exercise on training data. Four trials are deliberately small. A lucky sampled winner is not proof that random search is generally superior to grid search; a fair comparison needs matched evaluation boundaries and a meaningful budget.',
  'from scipy.stats import loguniform\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import RandomizedSearchCV, StratifiedKFold\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_pipeline\nd = make_splits()\ns = RandomizedSearchCV(make_pipeline(), {"model__C": loguniform(0.01, 100), "model__class_weight": [None, "balanced"]}, n_iter=4, random_state=47, cv=StratifiedKFold(3, shuffle=True, random_state=48), scoring="average_precision", n_jobs=1, error_score="raise")\ns.fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Sampled candidates:", len(s.cv_results_["params"]))\nprint("Chosen C:", round(s.best_params_["model__C"], 4))\nprint("Inner AP:", round(s.best_score_, 3))',
  'Repeat a seeded draw from the same log-uniform distribution.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import loguniform\na = loguniform(0.01, 100).rvs(size=4, random_state=47)\nb = loguniform(0.01, 100).rvs(size=4, random_state=47)\nprint("Repeated:", bool(np.array_equal(a, b)))\nprint("In range:", bool(((a >= 0.01) & (a <= 100)).all()))',
  'List four conditions for a useful comparison of search methods.',
  'for condition in ["same prediction task", "same evaluation boundaries", "declared objective", "recorded compute/trial budget"]:\n    print(condition)',
  [('What does n_iter=4 specify?', 'Four sampled candidates', 'Four outer holdouts', 'Exactly four model fits including all CV', 'Each candidate is itself fitted across the inner folds.'),
   ('Does a seed prove the sampled winner is globally optimal?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for log-uniform sampling', 'Reproducibility and optimality are different properties.')],
  'Compare lucky winners under different objectives and budgets.', 'Record the space, sampling budget and independent evaluation design.',
  'The selected C is a sampled configuration, and the displayed AP is inner selection feedback.')

L(1, '4. Keep adaptive TPE trials inside outer evaluation',
  'Run an actual small Optuna search without letting outer validation guide its trials.',
  [('Optuna study; trial; objective', 'A study records optimization trials. The objective fits and scores a candidate using only the current outer fitting portion.'),
   ('TPESampler', 'Tree-structured Parzen Estimator: an adaptive Bayesian-style sampler that models promising versus other parameter regions. It is not a Gaussian-process optimizer.'),
   ('suggest_float; suggest_categorical', 'Declare numerical and discrete trial choices. log=True samples a positive numerical range logarithmically.')],
  'Adaptive search uses previous trial outcomes to propose later candidates. That makes trial validation feedback part of training the selection procedure. Outer validation must remain outside the objective function and outside the sampler history.\n\nCreate a fresh study inside each outer fold. Otherwise trials from a previous fold may carry information from rows that the next fold intends to hold out. This tiny demonstration uses two outer folds, two inner folds and four trials per fresh study; it teaches boundaries, not a recommended optimization budget.\n\nThe first two TPE trials use startup sampling, then the sampler can adapt. We select AP in this standalone exercise. The main cost-based milestone remains its predeclared grid-and-threshold procedure; no comparison winner is chosen from the final holdout.',
  'import numpy as np\nimport optuna\nfrom sklearn.metrics import average_precision_score\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import StratifiedKFold, cross_val_score\nfrom lab import make_splits, make_pipeline\noptuna.logging.set_verbosity(optuna.logging.WARNING)\nd = make_splits()\nX, y = d["X_train"], d["y_train"]\nouter = StratifiedKFold(2, shuffle=True, random_state=47)\nfor fold, (fit, valid) in enumerate(outer.split(X, y), 1):\n    Xf, yf = X.iloc[fit], y.iloc[fit]\n    inner = StratifiedKFold(2, shuffle=True, random_state=48)\n    def objective(trial):\n        C = trial.suggest_float("C", 0.1, 10, log=True)\n        weight = trial.suggest_categorical("weight", [None, "balanced"])\n        return float(np.mean(cross_val_score(make_pipeline(C, weight), Xf, yf, cv=inner, scoring="average_precision", n_jobs=1, error_score="raise")))\n    study = optuna.create_study(direction="maximize", sampler=optuna.samplers.TPESampler(seed=47 + fold, n_startup_trials=2))\n    study.optimize(objective, n_trials=4)\n    best = study.best_params\n    fitted = make_pipeline(best["C"], best["weight"]).fit(Xf, yf)\n    ap = average_precision_score(y.iloc[valid], fitted.predict_proba(X.iloc[valid])[:, 1])\n    print("Outer fold", fold, "trials", len(study.trials), "AP", round(ap, 3))',
  'Create an empty in-memory study and inspect its objective direction.',
  'import optuna\noptuna.logging.set_verbosity(optuna.logging.WARNING)\ns = optuna.create_study(direction="maximize", sampler=optuna.samplers.TPESampler(seed=47))\nprint("Direction:", s.direction.name)\nprint("Completed trials:", len(s.trials))',
  'Identify the rows an objective may inspect in one outer fold.',
  'allowed = {"outer fitting rows": True, "outer validation labels": False, "reserved final labels": False}\nfor name, usable in allowed.items():\n    print(name, usable)',
  [('Should one study carry trial history across different outer folds?', 'No; create a fresh study per fold', 'Always', 'Only when using AP', 'Shared trial history can cross the intended information boundary.'),
   ('Do four TPE trials prove the optimum was found?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with startup trials', 'This small budget demonstrates an actual algorithm, not global optimality.')],
  'Let adaptive trials inspect outer validation scores while claiming those scores are untouched.', 'Adapt inside each outer fitting portion and evaluate the selected procedure afterward.',
  'Each printed outer score follows a separate four-trial study. Verbosity controls informational logging, not warning or error handling.')

L(1, '5. Treat the decision threshold as a learned choice',
  'Keep threshold selection inside candidate fitting and understand negative cost scorers.',
  [('make_scorer; greater_is_better=False', 'Wraps a lower-is-better loss as a scorer whose returned value is negated, because searches maximize scores.'),
   ('TunedThresholdClassifierCV; lab.make_tuned', 'Selects a binary decision threshold using internal validation. Our helper wraps the entire imputation/scaling/model pipeline, then refits it on its permitted input rows.')],
  'A model can rank cases reasonably yet make costly decisions at 0.5. The project assigns illustrative cost 1 to a false positive and 8 to a missed cancellation. These costs define the decision objective; they are not actual business prices.\n\nThreshold selection also learns from labels. In the milestone, parameter-search validation evaluates candidates whose threshold was selected using only that candidate\'s fitting rows. This adds a third level beneath outer evaluation and parameter selection. The complete refit rule is part of the algorithm being assessed.\n\nThe declared grid includes 0 through 1 and 1.01. The last value explicitly allows a never-flag option even if a numerical probability equals 1. Coarse thresholds limit resolution; they keep this teaching budget manageable. A cost of 4.5 becomes a scorer of -4.5, so maximizing the scorer minimizes cost.',
  TUNED47 + 'from lab import COST_SCORER\nprint("Selected threshold:", round(m.best_threshold_, 3))\nprint("Refit rows:", int(m.estimator_.named_steps["scale"].n_samples_seen_))\nprint("Training scorer nonpositive:", COST_SCORER(m, d["X_train"], d["y_train"]) <= 0)\nprint("Final holdout used here:", False)',
  'Calculate average loss for one false positive and one false negative.',
  'from lab import decision_cost\ncost = decision_cost([0, 1], [1, 0])\nprint("Mean cost:", cost)\nprint("Maximized scorer:", -cost)',
  'Inspect the declared threshold grid and internal fold count before fitting.',
  'from lab import make_tuned\nt = make_tuned()\nprint("Thresholds:", t.thresholds.round(2).tolist())\nprint("Internal folds:", t.cv.n_splits)',
  [('What scorer corresponds to a loss of 4.5?', '-4.5', '4.5', '0.5', 'The negative sign makes a lower loss rank higher.'),
   ('May a candidate tune its threshold using its parameter-validation labels?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with a pipeline', 'That validation must evaluate the already selected candidate procedure.')],
  'Tune the threshold on evaluation answers and present its cost as independent.', 'Select thresholds within fitting boundaries and evaluate the whole selection procedure.',
  'The reported training score is explicitly training feedback. Independent outer evaluation follows in the next part.')

L(1, '6. Evaluate the complete nested search',
  'Read outer costs as evidence about a declared selection procedure, not a menu of models to cherry-pick.',
  [('lab.make_search; nested_evaluate', 'The search compares six parameter combinations. Each candidate includes threshold CV; three outer folds evaluate that entire search with fresh fits.'),
   ('Outer selected parameters', 'Each outer fit may choose different settings. This is expected when evaluating a selection algorithm on different fitting samples.')],
  'The complete nesting is outer evaluation → parameter-selection folds → threshold-selection folds → fitted preparation and coefficients. Each level receives only the rows permitted by its parent. A pipeline alone does not create these evaluation boundaries; the split and search composition do.\n\nRead positive costs by negating test_cost, because the scorer stores negative loss. The horizontal line is the mean across these three equally sized validation folds. Fold spread is descriptive; three values are not automatically a confidence interval.\n\nDo not deploy whichever outer estimator happened to score lowest. After assessing the fixed procedure, run that same declared search on the full training portion, freeze its result and inspect the final holdout once. If outer feedback inspires a redesign, acknowledge that the redesigned procedure needs fresh evaluation.',
  'import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom lab import make_splits, nested_evaluate\nd = make_splits()\nr = nested_evaluate(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\ncosts = -r["test_cost"]\nprint("Outer costs:", costs.round(3).tolist())\nprint("Mean:", round(float(np.mean(costs)), 3))\nfor fold, search in enumerate(r["estimator"], 1):\n    print("Fold", fold, search.best_params_)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.3, 3.1))\nax.bar(["Outer 1", "Outer 2", "Outer 3"], costs, color="#247ba0")\nax.axhline(costs.mean(), color="#cf6e27", linestyle="--", label="Mean outer cost")\nax.set(ylabel="Illustrative cost per case", title="Held-out evaluation of the complete search")\nax.legend()\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Inspect candidate count and parameter-selection folds.',
  'from sklearn.model_selection import ParameterGrid\nfrom lab import make_search\ns = make_search()\nprint("Candidates:", len(ParameterGrid(s.param_grid)))\nprint("Parameter folds:", s.cv.n_splits)\nprint("Threshold folds:", s.estimator.cv.n_splits)',
  'Describe the correct next fit after this predeclared outer evaluation.',
  'steps = ["Repeat the same search on full training", "Freeze settings, learned state and threshold", "Report the reserved final holdout", "Document limits before any later redesign"]\nfor step in steps:\n    print(step)',
  [('Should you simply choose the lowest-cost outer estimator for release?', 'No; fit the declared procedure on full training', 'Always', 'Only when folds differ', 'Outer folds evaluate the procedure; they are not a new selection set.'),
   ('What does negating test_cost recover?', 'Positive mean decision loss', 'A confidence interval', 'Final holdout accuracy', 'The search scorer uses negative loss for maximization.')],
  'Choose a lucky outer fit or repeatedly redesign from outer scores while calling them untouched.', 'Evaluate the declared procedure, then perform its specified full-training fit.',
  'The bars describe different outer fits of the same procedure. They do not establish actual customer savings or an uncertainty interval.',
  figure=('Three outer fold costs for the complete parameter-and-threshold search', 'Every outer fold performs its own internal parameter and threshold selection. The dashed line is a descriptive mean, not a confidence interval.'))
